{"answer":"Requests automatically follows redirects because the library is designed to be a high‑level HTTP client that abstracts the low‑level HTTP protocol details from the user.  In the docs ("docs/user/quickstart.rst") the default behaviour is explicitly described: "By default Requests will perform location redirection …" and the "allow_redirects" flag is set to "True" for all verbs except "HEAD".  This convenience removes the burden from the user to inspect the status code, read the "Location" header, and resend the request manually.

The implementation in "src/requests/sessions.py" further demonstrates this intent.  The method "resolve_redirects" is called automatically when "allow_redirects" is true (see the comment "# Resolve redirects if allowed." on line 720).  It handles updating the request, preserving cookies, stripping or retaining authentication, following RFC rules for method changes, and maintaining a retry history ("resp.history").  These steps would otherwise be a tedious manual process.

In short, automatic redirect handling is provided to simplify typical use cases, reduce boilerplate code, and ensure consistent, RFC‑compliant behaviour across all requests made through the library."}

